        },
        {
            "id": "4543007",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "17:43, 29 April 2010",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(3,766 bytes)",
            "change": 52,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  fixing bolded step.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "# Understand color coding of conductors in the cordset.  If the dryer cord does have colored wires, white is the neutral wire to the center connector, the red and black wires are the hot line 1 and line 2 wires which connect to the 2 outer connections of the dryer terminal block. Don't forget the ground strap or green wire jumper from the center connector to the dryer frame."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Understand that if the dryer cord does have colored wires, white is the neutral wire to the center connector, the red and black wires are the hot live wires which connect to the 2 outer connections of the dryer terminal block, don't forget the ground strap or green wire jumper from the center connector to the dryer frame."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "4542998",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "17:41, 29 April 2010",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(3,714 bytes)",
            "change": 27,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  correcting some typos and grammar.)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "cleanup",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#  Obtain a cordset with the same electrical rating and number of slots as the receptical. If purchasing a cordset by matching the electrical rating of the receptical; remembering the slot pattern will not matter.  Most hardware stores and home supply centers offer cordsets for electric ranges and dryers with either 3 or 4 conductors for under $25.  These cordsets may look similar, but are not interchangeable due to different electrical ratings."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#  Obtain a cordset with the same electrical rating and number of slots.  If purchasing by matching the electrical rating of the receptical; remembering the slot pattern will not matter.  Most hardware stores and home supply centers offer cordsets for electric ranges and dryers with either 3 or 4 conductors for under $25.  These cordsets may look similar, but are not interchangeable due to different electrical ratings."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "4542982",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "17:38, 29 April 2010",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(3,687 bytes)",
            "change": -72,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#  Obtain a cordset with the same electrical rating and number of slots.  If purchasing by matching the electrical rating of the receptical; remembering the slot pattern will not matter.  Most hardware stores and home supply centers offer cordsets for electric ranges and dryers with either 3 or 4 conductors for under $25.  These cordsets may look similar, but are not interchangeable due to different electrical ratings.",
                    "# Connect the cordset to the terminal block at the rear of the dryer.  The cordset selected should have only 3 wires (line 1, line 2 and neutral; no ground wire). The center wire of this cable is the neutral wire and the outer wires are the \"hot\" line 1 and line 2 wires.  These hot wires connect to the outer 2 connections the power terminal block of the dryer, does not matter which is which as long as one hot is connected to each of the outer terminal connectors."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#  Obtain a cordset with the same electrical rating and number of slots.  If purchasing by matching the electrical rating of the receptical; remembering the slot pattern will not matter.  Most hardware stores and home supply centers offer cordsets for electric ranges and dryers with 3 and 4 conductors for under $20.  These codsets may look similar, but are not interchangeable due to different electrical ratings.",
                    "# Connect the cordset to the terminal block at the rear of the dryer.  The cordset selected should have only 3 wires (line 1, line 2 and neutral; no ground wire. The center wire of this cable is the neutral wire and the outer wires are the \"hot\" line 1 and line 2 wires.  The hot wires connect to the outer 2 connections the power terminal block of the dryer, does not matter which is which as long as one hot is connected to each of the outer terminal connectors. (each outer wire is 110-120 volts which, when added, gives you the 220-240 volts."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "4542971",
            "user": "Stevecon",
            "timestamp": "17:34, 29 April 2010",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(3,759 bytes)",
            "change": 148,
            "summary": "(Cleaned this article up for clarity)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Modern [[Locate Instruction Manuals for Older Kitchen Appliances|appliances]] require 4 conductor cordsets (separate conductors or wires for line 1, line 2, ground and neutral). Generally, this setup is safer, because the current-carrying neutral is not connected to the dryer case. However, it is useless if the wall receptacle does not have a separate ground slot. In that case, you'll have to do the very simple procedure of converting a 4-wire dryer to a 3-wire setup.  Conversion of these appliances in the U.S. is prohibited by the NEC (National Electric Code).  The receptical and wiring should be modernized to a 4 conductor arrangement instead.",
                    "#  Verify that the dryer requires a 240 volt / 30 amp supply.",
                    "#  Check that any existing dryer receptical has this same rating and number of slots (either 3 or 4 slots).",
                    "#  Obtain a cordset with the same electrical rating and number of slots.  If purchasing by matching the electrical rating of the receptical; remembering the slot pattern will not matter.  Most hardware stores and home supply centers offer cordsets for electric ranges and dryers with 3 and 4 conductors for under $20.  These codsets may look similar, but are not interchangeable due to different electrical ratings.",
                    "# Connect the cordset to the terminal block at the rear of the dryer.  The cordset selected should have only 3 wires (line 1, line 2 and neutral; no ground wire. The center wire of this cable is the neutral wire and the outer wires are the \"hot\" line 1 and line 2 wires.  The hot wires connect to the outer 2 connections the power terminal block of the dryer, does not matter which is which as long as one hot is connected to each of the outer terminal connectors. (each outer wire is 110-120 volts which, when added, gives you the 220-240 volts.",
                    "#  Notice that  the center wire (neutral wire) of the cordset goes to the center connector.  Since in this case the neutral serves as a neutral and a ground connection, a jumper conductor (ground strap) is also connected to the center terminal and to the frame of the dryer. A green wire could also be used to connect the center connector to the frame of the dryer."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Modern [[Locate Instruction Manuals for Older Kitchen Appliances|appliances]] come with separate ground and neutral wires. Generally, this setup is safer, because the current-carrying neutral is not connected to the dryer case. However, it is useless if the wall receptacle does not have a separate ground slot. In that case, you'll have to do the very simple procedure of converting a 4-wire dryer to a 3-wire setup.",
                    "",
                    "3 wire Connections NO LONGER Meet NEC Code (since 1996)",
                    "You should upgrade to a 4 wire connection & circuit",
                    "#  Identify the type of plug that you will need by looking at the \"slot pattern\" of the receptacle on the wall. You'll need to buy a new power cord/plug from an appliance or hardware store (~$12-$15).",
                    "# Look to see that the new cord (at the dryer end) will have 3 wires only, no ground wire. The center wire of this cable is the neutral wire and the outer wires are hot live wires.  The hot wires connect to the outer 2 connections the power terminal block of the dryer, does not matter which is which as long as one hot is connected to each of the outer terminal connectors. (each outer wire is 110-120 volts which, when added, gives you the 220-240 volts.",
                    "#(NOTE/CORRECTION - it's not clear or may be even a mistake, since the main wires in the plug are THE ZERO, and the other one is THE PHASE which voltage is a wave with active voltage - i.e. the analog of the DC Voltage of the same average power - of 110 Volts. So the voltage ZERO-PHASE and the GROUND-PHASE is the same 110 Volts. Voltage ZERO - GROUND is zero Volts, but according to the explanation of the technician, it is to be 110 too, which is not correct!)",
                    "#Notice that  the center wire (neutral wire) of the cable goes to the center connector. Since in this case the neutral serves as a neutral and a ground connection, a jumper band (ground strap) is also connected to the center terminal and to the frame of the dryer. A green wire could also be used to connect the center connector to the frame of the dryer."
                ]
            }
        },
